{"kind":"artifact","text":"Every MCP tool advertises an outputSchema, and it deliberately promises almost nothing. Exact clean pushed head 284f9002139d0c5beb44c8696f414c7976bb1cc2 on request/mcp-output-schema; tree f3907cb3, checkout clean, origin matching, parent is current main ebdca9fa, all verified at filing. Under planner's request 206708ca and my promise 9c20ee34, both live and unstale at filing.\n\nWHY THIS RESTS ON THE PROMISE ALONE. My previous artifact 2008eba8 named 87672c8a, the cmd/gitseq-mcp artifact the completion merge published at current main, as its predecessor at the path. That artifact is stale, so mine was stale from birth and codex correctly refused to promise a review against it. The dead-basis override records that I saw the staleness; it does not make the chain current, and I should not have reached for it when the effect was to hand a reviewer an unusable filing. There is no unstale artifact at cmd/gitseq-mcp standing at current main, so this one names no path predecessor rather than manufacturing a fresh-looking but wrong basis.\n\nTHE SCHEMA IS {\"type\":\"object\",\"additionalProperties\":true} ON ALL FOURTEEN TOOLS, attached by a loop over the definitions rather than repeated in each, so a tool added later cannot forget it.\n\nNO KEY IS NAMED, and the restraint is the design. Declaring an outputSchema makes conformant clients validate structuredContent against it and reject what does not conform, so a wrong schema is strictly worse than none: none degrades to today's behaviour, a wrong one breaks a working client. No key could be proved present on every return path — every resident-backed tool has a degraded local-fold fallback returning a differently shaped value, and submit conditionally adds degraded, warning and projected — so none is named.\n\nWHY type object IS ARGUED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Every success path in dispatch returns either a Go map or a statusview value struct — ActorStatus, WaitDelta, WorkPage, ArtifactPage, ItemInspection — and both encode as JSON objects. The failure path carries no structuredContent at all, so there is nothing there to validate. The envelope distinction was checked on the wire: the result's keys are _meta, content, isError, live_attention, resultType and structuredContent, so isError and live_attention are siblings rather than members.\n\nTHE MOTIVATING PREMISE HAS BEEN DISPROVED BY THE PERSON WHO REPORTED IT, and this artifact states the justification that survives rather than the one the request assumed. Hugh researched the client that prompted the row: the version of kiro he was using has no support for structuredContent at all, whether or not an outputSchema is advertised. So this change would not have fixed the case that produced the report. What stands on its own is narrower and still real — the MCP specification provides outputSchema for exactly this declaration, gitseq returns structuredContent while saying nothing about its shape, and a client that does gate on the schema's presence now has what it needs. That is conformance and interoperability, not a repair of an observed breakage, and hugh's judgement is that it is worth doing on those terms.\n\nTHE VERIFICATION CONDITION IS UNSATISFIABLE RATHER THAN UNDONE. The request asks for confirmation from a real client that structuredContent is now recognised. Hugh's words are that we do not know any environment that would positively confirm it. An MCP-process restart would not change that: the fourteen adapter processes running here are built from main at best, and this change is not in main, so restarting them yields a build without the schemas; and a build of this head would still meet no client known to surface the payload. What was verified is that a binary built from this head, driven over stdio against the real workroom, returns fourteen tools each carrying an object-typed outputSchema and a conforming whoami payload.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE. Skipping one tool in the attach loop fails TestStatelessDiscoverAndToolList by name, reporting the tool that declares no object outputSchema. Restored and green.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit, exit codes captured directly rather than through a pipe. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok, zero failures, zero cached. npm test 132 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet and git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"284f9002139d0c5beb44c8696f414c7976bb1cc2","path":"cmd/gitseq-mcp"}}